in today's morning rounds how heart  disease is a major risk for pregnant  women today is the American Heart  Association's Go Red For Women day heart  disease kills nearly 400 20,000 women in  the u.s. each year it's the leading  cause of death among women the disease  is the number one cause of maternal  deaths a recent study found about one in  four pregnancy or postpartum deaths are  caused by cardiovascular complications  our dr. taryn and Rula who's a  cardiologist spoke to one woman who had  a potentially life-threatening heart  complication during her pregnancy dr.  nurullah good morning good morning  Jericka the mortality numbers are even  more staggering for african-american  mothers their risk of dying from heart  related complications is three times  higher than white women we spoke to a  first-time mother who was diagnosed with  hypertension during her pregnancy and  once she learned her condition could  lead to serious future complications she  changed her life around this was  Michelle amiibo in 2014 she and her  husband Marcus were expecting their  first child just like all new parents  they worried about the health of their  baby but amiibo received unexpected news  in her third trimester during routine  visit with her obstetrician about her  own health she told me that my blood  pressure was extremely high amiibo says  that moment changed her life forever  and it was pretty alarming when I went  back the following week and my pressure  was still high that's when I knew there  was something wrong amiibo was diagnosed  with gestational hypertension it's also  known as high blood pressure that  develops during pregnancy after 20 weeks  in severe cases it can cause  preeclampsia or eclampsia a pregnancy  complication that can be fatal to the  baby and the mother hypertensive  disorders can affect as many as 1 in 10  women and can increase risk of heart  failure heart attack and stroke I really  don't know what caused the hypertension  so I know I had gained a lot of weight  the only thing I knew was that I had  hypertension and then hopefully after  giving birth to my daughter everything  will go back to normal and it just did  not four months after giving birth to  her daughter's suraíh amiibo struggled  to get her blood pressure under control  her  weight skyrocketed and her new normal  was chronic postpartum hypertension and  a daily blood pressure pill you didn't  expect to have a diagnosis of postpartum  hypertension no and I didn't even know  there was a thing called postpartum  hypertension risk factors for  cardiovascular disease related maternal  mortality are race ethnicity age  hypertension and obesity women who are  diagnosed with hypertension during  pregnancy are then at a higher risk for  high blood pressure and cardiovascular  disease in the future the number of  maternal mortality --zz in the u.s. is  actually increasing dr. Kathryn Weinberg  is a cardiologist at Lenox Hill Hospital  we're seeing that there's actually an  increase in the number of heart attacks  during pregnancy and that number is  going up in the United States we don't  exactly understand why we also sometimes  unmask disorders that we didn't realize  were present before you're doing great  amiibo got serious about her health she  works out at least five days a week has  cut sodium out of her diet and portion  controls all her meals do you feel like  having your daughter helped push you and  motivate you oh absolutely yeah I want  to be healthy for my daughter but it was  also - for me I'm not just the rise mom  I'm a show - and I have to take care of  myself she worked hard for four years to  lose 75 pounds and since last winter she  no longer needs blood pressure medicine  when you think of heart disease you  think of heart attack you think of  stroke hypertension is a real issue and  cardiovascular disease while pregnant is  a real issue we have to get the  information out there for moms and for  families Michelle amiibo is now a  spokeswoman for the American Heart  Association and hopes her story will  help other women know the risks you know  one of the things she said to me was I  can't believe I was chosen to be a  spokeswoman because all I had was  gestational hypertension I didn't have a  heart attack or stroke and that just  conveys the idea that most women don't  really think this is a big deal and dr.  Narula what are some of the signs of  that hypertension and what should one do  we saw Michelle was able to lose a lot  of weight and she's eating healthier but  what what should people do well if  you're diagnosed certainly you need  close follow-up during pregnancy and  then I think one of the big things is  have  after pregnancy you really need to meet  with a cardiologist in three months or  so to go over all of your risk factors  you need to know your numbers the issue  is during pregnancy and even in general  hypertension can be silent so you may  not feel anything but certainly in  pregnancy sometimes it's hard to tease  out you may feel short of breath or have  leg swelling and you just think that's  normal pregnancy but sometimes it can be  a marker of a bigger problem so you need  to speak up and speak to your OB and  potentially you know get referred  further on to a cardiologist even women  the way Michelle is responded is so  impressive I mean are there ways to  prevent heart disease during pregnancy  well there's ways to prevent heart  disease in general we try to educate  people that 80% 80% of cardiovascular  disease is preventable so things like as  you saw diet and exercise and stress  management all of these things can go a  really long way Tara thank you very much  very impressive story
 

